@page "/preview/printer"
@using Restaurant.UI.Shared.Services.Printing
@inject UnavailableReceiptPrinter NoPrinter

@* A development preview of a MAUI screen, not a back-office destination.

   Part II-A · Printer setup is a terminal screen and it stays one: a Bluetooth
   bond is a fact about one radio and one piece of glass, so the pairing surface
   has to be on the device that holds the radio. This route exists so the screen
   can be worked on without an emulator, in the same way /preview/terminal
   exists. It ships to no venue.

   **This preview cannot pair anything and does not pretend to.** It is handed
   UnavailableReceiptPrinter explicitly — the answer §12 calls for, in the same
   shape as UnknownDeviceStatus: it finds nothing, selects nothing, refuses to
   print, and says the refusal is about this host rather than about the printer.

   The override is not decoration. This host now registers real transports for the
   back office at /printers, so without it, opening this preview would start a scan
   of the venue's network and the screen would stop being the state this page
   exists to show. The preview shows a terminal before anybody has paired anything,
   which is a different screen from the back office's and stays one. What is on
   screen below is therefore the real no-printer state, drawn by the real
   component, and it is the state a terminal is in before anybody has paired
   anything.

   The separate route rather than a MORE tap inside /preview/terminal is
   deliberate: the terminal preview is a frame at a chosen device size and
   navigating it would hide the screen this page exists to show. *@

<PageTitle>Printer setup preview</PageTitle>

<section class="panel pp">

    <header class="pp__head">
        <p class="pp__kicker">DEVELOPMENT PREVIEW &middot; MAUI SCREEN</p>
        <h1 class="pp__title">Terminal &middot; Printer setup</h1>
        <p class="pp__note">
            The <code>PrinterSetupScreen</code> <code>Restaurant.Mobile</code> renders behind
            <code>MORE</code>, stood up at the terminal&rsquo;s 1440&times;900. This host registers
            <code>UnavailableReceiptPrinter</code> to this screen, so every control below is the
            honest no-printer state and nothing here can reach a printer. The back office&rsquo;s own
            printer destination is <code>/printers</code>, and it is a different screen doing a
            different job.
        </p>
    </header>

    <div class="pp__frame">
        <div class="pp__device">
            <TerminalShell ActiveDestination="more">
                <PrinterSetupScreen TerminalId="Counter 2" PrinterOverride="NoPrinter" />
            </TerminalShell>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* The handheld width, because the screen has a container-query step of its
       own and a step nobody looks at is a step that breaks. Same rule the
       terminal preview follows: two shells at two widths in one browser window,
       both correct at once, because the query asks the shell how wide it is and
       not the viewport. *@
    <p class="pp__kicker">393 &times; 785 &middot; handheld &middot; provisional</p>

    <div class="pp__frame pp__frame--handheld">
        <div class="pp__device pp__device--handheld">
            <TerminalShell ActiveDestination="more">
                <PrinterSetupScreen TerminalId="Counter 2" PrinterOverride="NoPrinter" />
            </TerminalShell>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
